  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nHow is the Minimum Corporate Income Tax (MCIT) computed and when does it apply?\n\n### Short Answer\nThe Minimum Corporate Income Tax (MCIT) is computed at a rate of 2% of the gross income as of the end of the taxable year, applicable starting from the fourth taxable year following the year in which the corporation commenced its business operations, provided that the MCIT exceeds the regular corporate income tax for that year.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\n- **RR 9-1998**: This regulation provides further details on the implementation of the MCIT.\n\n### Legal Interpretation\n\n### Practical Application\nCorporations should monitor their income levels closely, especially as they approach the fourth year of operations, to determine if the MCIT will apply. If the MCIT exceeds the regular corporate income tax, the corporation must pay the MCIT. Any excess of the MCIT over the regular income tax can be carried forward and credited against the normal income tax for the next three taxable years. This provision allows corporations to manage their tax liabilities effectively, especially in the early years of operation when profits may be lower.",
